---
description: MeetUp2 Remote is a native macOS app that controls the zoom and pan/tilt of a connected Logitech MeetUp 2 camera over USB.
---

***definitions***

- :MeetUp2Remote: is a native macOS menu bar app that controls the zoom and pan/tilt of a connected Logitech MeetUp 2 camera over USB.
  - It lives in the macOS menu bar; clicking its menu bar icon opens a small panel with the camera controls.
  - It has no Dock icon.
  - It controls a single MeetUp 2 at a time; when several are attached, it controls the first one it detects and ignores the others.

- :MeetUpCamera: represents the Logitech MeetUp 2 camera attached to the Mac over USB and controlled by :MeetUp2Remote:. :MeetUpCamera: has:
  - Connection state - connected or disconnected (required)
  - Current zoom value - read from the camera (available while connected)
  - Zoom has a valid range with a minimum and maximum reported by the camera itself.
  - Pan and tilt movement - the camera moves by relative movement commands and does not report a pan or tilt position.

- :ControlPanel: is the small panel of :MeetUp2Remote: that opens from the menu bar icon and contains the controls for :MeetUpCamera:.

- :CameraControlLink: is the component of :MeetUp2Remote: that reads and adjusts the zoom of :MeetUpCamera: and moves its pan and tilt through relative movement commands over USB.

***implementation reqs***

- :Implementation: should be in Swift.

- :Implementation: should target macOS 14 as the minimum deployment version.

- :Implementation: should use SwiftUI for the user interface, with the menu bar icon and :ControlPanel: implemented as a MenuBarExtra scene.

- :CameraControlLink: should communicate with :MeetUpCamera: using Apple's IOKit USB APIs, sending standard USB Video Class (UVC) control requests (GET_CUR, GET_MIN, GET_MAX, SET_CUR) to the camera's video control interface for the zoom, pan, and tilt controls. No third-party USB or UVC libraries should be used.

- :Implementation: should be a Swift Package Manager project with a Package.swift manifest, built with `swift build` and tested with `swift test`; the app is an executable target.

- :Implementation: should be organized in two layers: a device layer containing :CameraControlLink: and camera detection behind a Swift protocol, and a UI layer containing the SwiftUI views and a single observable view model that mediates between :ControlPanel: and the device layer.
  - The protocol seam allows substituting a fake camera implementation in tests.
  - The layers are introduced incrementally: each layer, protocol, and component exists only from the first functionality that needs it onward; earlier functionalities do not scaffold them in advance.

- Camera detection should identify :MeetUpCamera: by matching USB vendor ID 0x046D (Logitech) and a USB product name containing "MeetUp".
  - Detection is successful only when the full communication chain is acquired: the matched USB device, its video control interface, and the camera terminal unit ID discovered per the UVC protocol resource referenced by the :CameraControlLink: requirement; matching the device alone does not count as connected.
  - Detection should enumerate USB devices via IOKit service matching on the `IOUSBHostDevice` service class (the class modern macOS registers for USB devices).
  - The vendor ID is read from the device's IORegistry property `idVendor` and the product name from the IORegistry property `USB Product Name` (via `IORegistryEntryCreateCFProperty`); the registry entry name must not be used for matching.
  - For reference, the live device reports `USB Product Name` = "Logi MeetUp 2" and `idVendor` = 0x046D.

- :CameraControlLink: must perform real UVC control transfers against the camera's video control interface for every zoom read (GET_CUR, GET_MIN, GET_MAX) and write (SET_CUR) and for every pan/tilt relative movement command.
  - Returning simulated, hard-coded, or placeholder values in place of actual device communication is not acceptable in the real device-layer implementation; only the fake camera used by tests may simulate values.
  - The interface addressing, request codes, control selectors, value layouts, and unit-ID discovery are defined in [uvc-control-protocol.yaml](resources/uvc-control-protocol.yaml).

- The device layer should log every UVC operation and its outcome to standard error for diagnostics: the request kind, control selector, unit ID, IOKit result code, the raw byte buffer in hexadecimal, and the parsed values read or written.
  - Every step of the camera detection chain is also logged with its outcome and IOKit result code: device matching (matched name and vendor ID), video control interface lookup, interface plug-in creation and open, and camera terminal unit ID discovery (including each probed unit ID and its result when the probe fallback is used).
  - When any detection step fails, the logged failure names the step, so a "not connected" outcome is always attributable to a specific step.
  - Diagnostic logging of failures on standard error is required, never suppressed.

- :UnitTests: should use the Swift Testing framework (`import Testing`, `@Test` functions) and live in the package's test target.

- :UnitTests: should exercise the view model and control logic against a fake camera implementation substituted through the device-layer protocol; they must not touch IOKit or real USB hardware.

***test reqs***

- :ConformanceTests: should be implemented using the Swift Testing framework (`import Testing`, `@Test` functions).

- :ConformanceTests: will be run using the `swift test` command.

- :ConformanceTests: must be implemented and executed - do not skip tests.

- :ConformanceTests: must not require a real camera, IOKit access, or a display; they exercise the specified behavior through the device-layer protocol using the fake camera implementation.
  - Exception: acceptance tests that explicitly state they run against a physical Logitech MeetUp 2 use the real device layer over USB; they require the camera to be attached and fail (never skip) when it is absent.

***functional specs***

- :MeetUp2Remote: has an entry point. When launched, its icon appears in the macOS menu bar and no Dock icon is shown.

- Clicking the menu bar icon opens :ControlPanel:; clicking it again closes :ControlPanel:.

- At launch, :MeetUp2Remote: detects the first attached Logitech MeetUp 2 and treats it as the :MeetUpCamera:.
  - :ControlPanel: displays the connection state of :MeetUpCamera:: "Connected" when a camera is found, "No camera found" otherwise.
  - Detection happens only at launch; attaching or detaching a camera while :MeetUp2Remote: is running takes effect only after the app is restarted.

  ***acceptance tests***

  - With a physical Logitech MeetUp 2 attached over USB, detection enumerates the complete USB device list, regardless of the order in which devices are yielded: the number of devices probed equals the number of USB devices an independent enumeration reports, every probed device has readable identity properties (a probe reporting an empty product name together with vendor ID 0x0000 is a failure), and the MeetUp 2 is found even when it is not the first device yielded.

- :CameraControlLink: reads the current zoom value of the connected :MeetUpCamera:, together with its valid range (minimum and maximum), and can issue relative pan and tilt movement commands.
  - When a read or adjustment command fails while the camera is connected, :ControlPanel: shows a short error message and the zoom value is re-read from the camera so the display stays truthful.

  ***acceptance tests***

  - With a physical Logitech MeetUp 2 attached over USB, :CameraControlLink: reads the zoom value and range from the live device (not from the fake camera): the zoom range minimum is strictly smaller than its maximum, the current zoom value lies within the range, and the device reports the relative pan/tilt movement control as supported.

- While :MeetUpCamera: is connected, :ControlPanel: displays its current zoom value as provided by :CameraControlLink:.
  - While no camera is connected, no value is displayed.

- :ControlPanel: contains a zoom slider.
  - The slider spans the zoom range reported by :CameraControlLink:.
  - The slider position reflects the current zoom value of :MeetUpCamera:.
  - Moving the slider sets the zoom of :MeetUpCamera: to the selected absolute value via :CameraControlLink:.
  - The slider is disabled while no camera is connected.

- :ControlPanel: contains a directional pad with up, down, left, and right buttons.
  - Pressing left or right moves the pan of :MeetUpCamera: by issuing one short relative movement pulse in that direction via :CameraControlLink:.
  - Pressing up or down moves the tilt of :MeetUpCamera: by issuing one short relative movement pulse in that direction via :CameraControlLink:.
  - A movement pulse starts the movement in the chosen direction and stops it after a short fixed duration; the camera enforces its own movement limits.
  - Issuing a movement pulse is a single operation whose completion can be awaited; the operation completes only after the stop command has been issued, so a caller that awaits it deterministically observes both the start and the stop.
  - No pan or tilt position is displayed or tracked.
  - The directional pad is disabled while no camera is connected.

- :ControlPanel: arranges its elements vertically, top to bottom: the connection state, the current zoom value, the zoom slider, and the directional pad.
